# C-type dipole yoke -- 1/4 xz model (x>=0 and z>=0), HEX yoke + tet air
#
# Variant of em_1-2_half_z.jou.  Both x=0 and z=0 become symmetry planes:
#   x=0:  B . n = 0 (flux parallel)       -> sym_bn=0_x (Radia '+x')
#   z=0:  H x n = 0 (flux perpendicular)  -> sym_ht=0_z (Radia '-z')
#
# Matches the ELF CEFC 2020 reference convention IMA='+x-z' for HDiv-VIM.
# For FEM Omega the solver picks:
#   sym_bn=0_x -> Natural (do nothing)
#   sym_ht=0_z -> Dirichlet (Omega = 0)
# For FEM A the solver picks the opposite assignment.
#
# This variant is a REGRESSION GUARD for the reduction pipeline plumbing,
# not a physics-accuracy comparison to any published reference.  The coil
# (em_sample_coil.py) is a full racetrack -- the symmetry BCs impose the
# field symmetry but do not "undo" the coil's actual spatial distribution.
# End users chasing the published ELF -228 mT reference need the full
# ELF pipeline (HDiv-VIM via calc_accel_hdiv.py + matching coil symmetry).
#
# Units: meters
#
# Coordinate system:
#   y = gap direction (pole tip at y~0, main leg at y~0.15)
#   z = beam direction (symmetry at z=0)
#   x = depth/thickness (symmetry at x=0)
#
# 3 yoke volumes (x>=0, z>=0 quadrant of the original C-yoke):
#   Vol 1: Pole piece  (x: [0, 12.5], y: [0, 40],   z: [0, 50]  mm)
#   Vol 2: Yoke back   (x: [0, 12.5], y: [40, 120], z: [0, 25]  mm)
#   Vol 3: Main leg    (x: [0, 12.5], y: [120, 182.5], z: [0, 52.5] mm)
#
# Use "Symmetry reduction" x=bn=0, z=ht=0 in the Export Mesh dialog.
reset

# === Pole piece (near gap) ===
# Reduced: x [0, 12.5], y [0, 40], z [0, 50] mm
brick x 0.0125 y 0.040 z 0.050
move volume 1 x 0.00625 y 0.020 z 0.025

# === Yoke back (horizontal return) ===
# Reduced: x [0, 12.5], y [40, 120], z [0, 25] mm
brick x 0.0125 y 0.080 z 0.025
move volume 2 x 0.00625 y 0.080 z 0.0125

# === Main leg (where coil sits) ===
# Reduced: x [0, 12.5], y [120, 182.5], z [0, 52.5] mm
brick x 0.0125 y 0.0625 z 0.0525
move volume 3 x 0.00625 y 0.15125 z 0.02625

# === Imprint/merge for shared surfaces (continuous mesh) ===
imprint volume all
merge volume all

# === Hex mesh ===
volume all scheme map
volume 1 size 0.005
volume 2 size 0.008
volume 3 size 0.006
mesh volume all

# === Air domain: 1/4 sphere (x>=0, z>=0 quadrant) ===
# Use boolean intersection with an octant brick (cleaner topology than
# webcut+delete for small quadrants; same technique that the reduction
# end-to-end test uses).
create sphere radius 0.40
create brick x 0.80 y 0.80 z 0.80
# Octant brick covers x: [0, 0.80], y: [-0.40, 0.40], z: [0, 0.80]
move volume 5 x 0.40 y 0 z 0.40

# Sphere - brick intersect = quarter-sphere (x>=0, z>=0)
intersect volume 4 5 keep
delete volume 4 5

# After intersect+delete, exactly one air volume remains -- subtract yoke
# from it.  The air volume ID is the next highest; identify by material.
# (Using parse_cubit_list here would be more robust, but the .jou stays
# ID-linear thanks to the deterministic Cubit allocation sequence.)
subtract volume 1 2 3 from volume 6 keep
imprint volume all
merge volume all
volume 6 scheme tetmesh
volume 6 size 0.02
mesh volume 6

# === Blocks ===
set duplicate block elements on
block 1 add volume 1 2 3
block 1 name "yoke"
block 2 add volume 6
block 2 name "air"

# Kelvin (FEM only): add via the launcher dialog "Add Kelvin open
# boundary (auto)" checkbox with "Symmetry reduction" x=bn0, z=ht0.
# The launcher writes a kelvin_reduction object into launcher_config.json
# (keys 'x' and 'z' with values 'bn0' and 'ht0' respectively), then plays
# auto_kelvin_entry.py which delegates to add_kelvin_cubit just before
# export netgen.  (APREPRO chokes on embedded braces + colons, so
# this comment avoids showing the literal JSON.)
